### Find commands

# To list files modified within the last 6 days
```bash
find . -type f -mtime -6
```
• . : Searches inside the current directory and all of its subdirectories.
• -type f : Restricts the search to files only (excluding directories).
• -mtime -6 : Filters for files modified less than 6 days ago (the minus sign indicates "less than").

1. Automating Cleanup & Maintenance
Instead of just listing files, you can use the -exec flag or xargs to automatically take action on them.
• Delete logs older than 30 days:
```bash
find /var/log -type f -mtime +30 -name "*.log" -delete
```
• Archive recent work: Compress files modified within the last 7 days into a backup tarball:
```bash
find . -type f -mtime -7 -exec tar -rvf backup.tar {} +
```
2. Troubleshooting & Security Auditing
When an application breaks unexpectedly, you often need to find out exactly what changed and when.
• Identify files changed in the last 60 minutes: Use -mmin (modification time in minutes) to catch immediate, recent changes:
```bash
find . -type f -mmin -60
```
• Detect files accessed but not changed: If you suspect data leakage or want to see what configuration files a process read in the last 24 hours, use -atime (access time):
```bash
find /etc -type f -atime -1
```
• Find files with permission or ownership changes: Use -ctime (change time) to track metadata modifications (like changing file permissions via chmod):
```bash
find . -type f -ctime -3
```
3. Date Ranges & Comparative Searches
You don't have to limit your search to a single threshold. You can combine operators to isolate specific brackets of time.
• Find files modified within a tight window: Find files that are older than 2 days but newer than 7 days:
```bash
find . -type f -mtime +2 -mtime -7
```
• Compare against a reference file: Find any file modified more recently than a specific reference file (e.g., your last known stable deployment file):
```bash
find . -type f -newer reference_deploy.txt
```
